Hi Tom- I don't know if there is anything relevant here, but NYSERNet was
concerned about negative publicity the Internet had been given lately, so
if a "popular press"  reporter asked for some connectivity to research a
story, we gave it. I walked several of them thru their first uses of the net.

One of these was Frank Bajak, of the AP International desk, who just filed
a series of stories in the last few weeks.

Another was Bill Broad of the NYT, whose piece on how scientists use the
Internet appeared in the NYTimes last Tues.

Another was Ira Flatow, of NPR, and many of you just heard the Science
Friday show devoted to that.

Yet another is Dan Dubno, a CBS producer, who started out researching a
brief story on the Internet, decided there was too much and expanded it to
a three day report, and now is starting to think about Internet: the
mini-series. (only kidding on that last part!)

What it costs NYSERNet is a little over $50 in connectivity, and my time
to help explain the net to these folks who are explaining it to the Rest
of the US. We can't give it away to everyone, but reporters with high
exposure have been successful in getting an account from us so far. 

We think it's worth it, and recommend it to others in a position to do the
same thing.

Best-
