In article <1993May16.032817.4214@sserve.cc.adfa.oz.au> tomw@ccadfa.cc.adfa.oz.au (Tom Worthington) writes:

> The network has asked the ACS to provide a demonstration of BBS
> Pornography live on national TV, 7am Thursday Morning. The ACS National
> office volunteered me as the "expert" (a dubious honour) on the subject.

Oh well... :-((

*Never* do that, in any case. This isn't a call for suppressing the
dissemination of valuable information but rather a plea to let
Pandora's box closed.

Let me explain why.

At the end of 1991, a renowned German computer magazine ran an article
about pornography on private BBSs. Nothing was said about childrens'
access or other problematic issues, such as illegal material. Just the
plain fact that there are "adult" areas in some of those BBSs. It was
a technical magazine, not mainstream press, and probably nobody would
have noticed it at all.

Apparently it was this article which prompted a German feminist
magazine, which runs a general anti-porn campaign, to "investigate"
into the issue and feature it themselves. They focused at universities
and asked some people there to show them porn pictures and stories
from newsgroups. Then they ran an article about "how German university
people use government funds for displaying porn stuff on their screens
during the day instead of working". Much blah-blah and almost nothing
which was technically true, of course, but...

This prompted a general outcry in the public and much pressure on
German universities to stop the practice. It took only a few weeks
until almost all of them censored their newsfeed. Some blocked
alt.sex.*, some blocked alt.* entirely, some blocked everything which
had *sex* or *erotica* in it, at least one took the opportunity to
"clean up" on "undesired" topics such as sci.military (of course, the
person responsible had never taken a look into sci.military).

Since the feminist paper explicitly asked its readership for telling
University administrations about this "problem" and specifically for
telling names of "offenders", many Universities got a lot of (mostly
anonymous) letters asking for removal of newsgroups or the firing of
specific people. I know of several cases where people were forced to
leave the University or threatened with losing their job if they would
ever post to certain newsgroups, sometimes on no other grounds than
posting to *technical* newsgroups.

This happens when you tell fanatics the truth about the wrong things.

The censorship of newsgroups is still in effect, and it has gotten
harder for students to justify their demand for network access, not to
tell about the general climate which has become more accepting to
censorship.

> * The prevalence of pornographic material on BBSs and other computer
> sources, such as the Internet (including suggested sources of material we
> could demonstrate),

I don't know about any source but Usenet, but: If you absolutely want
to demonstrate, show the media people at most an actual article in one
of the appropriate newsgroups, with header and the start of an
uu-encoded picture. Tell them that these seemingly random numbers need
a complicated process of saving them to files, cutting out headers,
combining the files in the right order, uu-decoding them and finding
the right view program. Tell them that the kind of computer expertise
needed to do that is simply too much for children. Tell them that this
could be a picture, but it could as well be a computer program, and
you need to know much to tell the difference. But please, do not show
those pictures to the press as pictures, in any way. 

Of course what the TV people think the people expect is a pornographic
picture showing up on the screen at less than three keystrokes. So
they will show pictures in the TV feature. They will hold a camera to
a Playboy centerfold, display the picture on a video screen, film the
video screen and claim that it was a computer screen. This is the way
news are made. But then you at least have a reason to complain
fiercely about those methods.

The alternative solution is to demand that either the show is
broadcast live, or that you had to approve the final cut before
airing. 

> * Any ways in which this material might be being censored, or its access
> controlled for children. Are there any laws, or accepted practices,
> covering it?

There are many *un*accepted practices, see above...

> * Opinions as to what should be done, if anything, about this. Is it
> really a problem? Does it create dangers for censorship of other material?

Okay. This is really not a problem, unless someone uses it to enforce
censorship. If the press gets a focus on what it considers a problem,
eventually *you* will get a problem unless the coverage concerns a
real problem in the first place. A call for publishing something about
this nasty business (I personally dislike it too, but I value freedom
of speech much, much higher) always implies a call for censorship,
because if you didn't consider it a problem, it would not be worth
spending air minutes about.

> I would like to avoid sensationalising the issue. However this is a matter

Then avoid to touch it at all - the press will do its work to
over-sensationalize it.

> of legitimate public interest. Sydney based ACS members who would like to

I think while it might, perhaps, be of marginal public interest, the
problems of making a big deal about it by far outweigh the benefits.
You inevitably will end up with a much bigger censorship problem than
with any pornography problem. Germany should be enough of an example.
:-(

Save the publicity for the really important issues. Perhaps you could
convince them to run an airing about the benefits of computer
networks, or if they are stuck to problems, about *real* computer
crime.

My 3 pfennigs worth of it,

olaf


