Good luck!

In my opinion, speaking as a system administrator who has had some
experience with malicious users, suspension from school is an
appropriate penalty for a student who has actually used a stolen
password to break into and use another person's account. 
  I also believe that it can be appropriate to penalize a student who is 
in possession of stolen passwords, such as the output from a password
cracking program. Our student code of conduct, which is a part of the
formal contract between student and university, prohibits "gaining or
seeking to gain unauthorized access" to another user's files.
  It is possible that, given due process, even possession of cracked
passwords might not be grounds for suspension. Our university provides
for a series of escalating penalties; possibly probation or some sort 
of warning would be a more appropriate treatement than suspension.
Personally, I think being in possession of stolen passwords is
comparable to being in possession of a stolen office key, which is a
crime here even if the office has not (yet) been ransacked.

  I do NOT think that simply possessing a passwd file or even a copy
of the crack software is grounds for punishment. This is all publicly
available and publicly readable and knowledge of how security software
works is relevant to a computer science education, as well as to a hot
topic in the news. 

  The crime is not possession of the software. The crime is invasion of
privacy and intrusion, which only happens when a cracked password is
USED. System administrators need to remember that. If users are told
in advance that running password cracking software is against the
rules, then there can be penalties attached to breaking the rules, as
with any other sort of policy.  

As you point  out, though, /etc/passwd has other uses besides cracking
passwords. It is ordinarily the only place on the system where a list 
of user names is kept in full. On a system with password shadowing, in
fact, the password file does not contain password information and
cannot be used to crack passwords. Having a list of user names is of
only marginal utility to a potential cracker. Punishing a user for
copying this file, which does not even contain password information,
is, well, extreme to the point of silliness. 
   
In addition to the other suggestion of getting a lawyer, I wonder if
the University would consent to mediating this case? If the university
officials could get together with their system staff and an outside
unix security person, they might be able to settle this without a
lawsuit.

Betsy

