I would like to add my two cents to this discussion.  I probably should
add, as a disclaimer, that I am not a student, and have not been one
for... well, longer than it feels like (am I really getting that old?).
So I am probably not up-to-date on the current policies at colleges these
days.  With that in mind, my views on what happened, as I understand it,
are as follows:

Not having access to the policy manual, I am going to assume that KC's
account was set up exclusively for the purpose of taking the class he
later dropped.  If not, then the entire argument for his case should be
that the college had no right to cancel his account when he dropped the
class.  If, however, it was not a general access account, but was
specifically for a particular class (as I believe it probably was), the
college had every right to cancel his account.

If the above is not the case, you can either ignore the rest of this post,
or choose to flame me to a crisp, because that is my main starting point.

I must say that I think two hours is not very realistic (or courteous) to
allow for cleaning out his files.  During my appeal, I think I would argue
that they should have allowed more time, even if only to allow me to do it
without having to drop everything I was doing, and rush through it without
being able to take the time to fully examine what I was actually purging.
I know it would take me much longer than that (two _days_, maybe), to
clear out my account here at work.  (Although I doubt whether such an
argument would make a lot of difference, I would through it in there
anyway.)

Assuming the college was justified in canceling KC's account, then he was
definitely in the wrong for printing out the /etc/passwd file.  And that
has nothing to do with whether or not he could decrypt passwords (the
college there must have some real winners employed as sys admins if it
takes then 5 to 10 minutes just to figure out that there are no passwords
in their /etc/passwd).  It has to do with what he was authorized to do on
the computer.

As soon as KC dropped the class, and particularly once he was informed
that his account was being canceled, he had no valid right to perform
general purpose activities on the computer.  The college could (possibly)
have handled the situation in the following way, and avoided letting KC
have the opportunity to print anything:  invalidate his password, possibly
setting up a message informing him that his account is canceled should he
try to log on; dump all files belonging to him to a diskette, possibly
using a DOS format for text files (determined by using the 'file' command,
NOT by looking at them); deleting all files dumped to diskette; giving the
diskette(s) to KC.  Note that I am NOT supporting this method, but I can
see arguments supporting it.

When KC was given the two hours (again, not long enough) to clean up his
account, the only activities that he had valid authority to perform were
to do just that: clean out his account.  By this time he had no further
rights to be on the computer, except for the sole purpose of removing his
personal files.  Printing the /etc/passwd file was definitely
"unauthorized use or misuse of any computer".  So would anything besides
find (searching for files belonging to himself), ls, pg/more/less (to look
at files belonging to himself), cpio/tar/backup/dd/whatever (to back up to
diskette/tape files belonging to himself, and rm (to remove files
belonging to himself.  Possibly mail, to let the sysadmin know that he was
finished.  I may have missed some other appropriate command, but you
should get my point.

Having said all that, let me say that although I think KC was in the wrong
for printing /etc/passwd, I definitely think the college was wrong in
setting punishment.  I would have gone along with expulsion for the rest
of the term, and _possibly_ a prohibition of computer usage for a year.
Based on the severity of the sentence, and the later admission that KC had
already been a thorn in their sides, I think they over-reacted, and were
much too harsh.

I do have a question for KC.  When you printed /etc/passwd, didn't you
stop to think about the possible consequences?  Or was it a rash action
brought on because you were rushed, and probably upset/angry?  You could
have gotten the same information, in a format that the college (probably)
could not have complained about, by executing:
	cut -f1,5 -d: /etc/passwd | lpr
The first part of this command is listed on the man page for cut on my
system.  Then you pipe it to the printer, and all you get are login names
and full names.  Or, you could use "-f5" instead of "-f1,5", and only get
a list of full names.  Better yet, you could have asked one of the
students remaining in the class to run it for you after everything was all
over.  Oh, well, so much for hindsight, huh?

Anyway, I guess what all of my ramblings boil down to is this: the college
was justified in canceling KC's account; the college should have given KC
longer to clean up his files; KC was in the wrong to print /etc/passwd;
the college was overly harsh in their sentencing.

