In article <1ttvirINN1pg@mojo.eng.umd.edu> (alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk,comp.admin.policy), tedwards@eng.umd.edu (Thomas Grant Edwards) writes:
> In closing, I have no respect for any person who writes a use
> policy where looking at /etc/passwd is illegal when it is
> world readable.  Period. 

In fact, such a policy is created by an institution only to create an atmosphere
in which it can, arbitrarily and at its sole discretion, apply punitive
measures to its members.

I specialize in commercial sites, where such a policy would be
laughable:  systems managers are expected to seal up any data that is
truly confidential (unlike that contained in a shadowed passwd file).  
As an earlier poster stated, in such an environment the systems
managers, perhaps more than the user, would be held accountable for 
any leaked information (if the leak was due to poor protection rather than
extremely clever or malicious hacking).

In academic sites, it seems to me that the idea is to promote the natural
curiosity and creativity of all involved:  students, faculty, and yes, even
staff.  That's why, when I worked at UC Irvine as a sys admin, I promoted
the creation of permanent undergraduate accounts (rather than class accounts
that come and go with each semester).  The most successful comp sci students
I have known spent more hours on the machine doing non-assigned work (even
playing "hack" occasionally 8^) than classwork.

OK, but then say that your school just doesn't have the resources in these
troubled times to support that kind of curiosity ("sorry, our library only
carries textbooks").  Even then, if somebody were using more resources than
appropriate for classwork, one would expect them to receive an explanation
and perhaps, for multiple offenses, a slap on the wrist.  And certainly, in
any case that involves severe disciplinary action, the accused individual
must be entitled to a hearing.  One would hope that the punishment would fit
the crime in such situations.  

The appropriate "punishment" for the "crime" of reading (intentionally)
publicly available system files is left as an exercise for the reader.  Hint:
 it doesn't involve expulsion.

Scott

