bdixon@wap.oau.org (Bill Dixon) writes:
>The college could (possibly) have handled the situation in the following 
>way, and avoided letting KC have the opportunity to print anything:  in-
>validate his password, possibly setting up a message informing him that 
>his account is canceled should he try to log on; dump all files belonging 
>to him to a diskette, possibly using a DOS format for text files (determined 
>by using the 'file' command, NOT by looking at them); deleting all files 
>dumped to diskette; giving the diskette(s) to KC.  Note that I am NOT 
>supporting this method, but I can see arguments supporting it.

You seem to be implying that this method is improper -- why?

I do something quite similar with routinely cancelled accounts; I've never
had to remove an account for disciplinary reasons.  My account deletion
process runs like this:

	- Compress files in to-be-deleted accounts.

	- Change login shell of cancelled accounts to 
	  /usr/local/bin/deadlogin, a shell script which
	  prints this message:

    Since you are no longer affiliated with the College of Engineering,
    your access to the Engineering Computer Center systems has been de-
    activated. 
    
    If you have any files which you want to keep or transfer to another
    system, contact the ECC staff in 280 Anderson Hall.  You may also
    use FTP to manually transfer your files to another system; if you
    do this, please let us know when you are finished, so we may remove
    your files.  Your files will be preserved until [target date]; after 
    that date, they will be placed in the ECC tape archives.

    If your access should be reactivated, contact the ECC staff.
    After your status with the College is verified, your access
    will be reinstated.

	- Wait until [target date].  This date depends on the time of
	  year; I usually set the date 6 weeks into the next full semes-
	  ter, ignoring summer sessions.

	- Archive all cancelled accounts to a single "deletions tape"
	  (i.e.  "Deletions - Spring 92", "Deletions - Fall 91", etc.)

	- Remove accounts.

I believe that this system works well.  The users (mostly students)
have a "grace period" of at least 6 weeks (longer in the summer) to 
discover that their access is gone and contact me for assistance in 
tranferring their files (or archiving them to user-provided media).  
This grace period also takes care of those who "fall through the
cracks", such as a graduating senior who decides to go to graduate
school.  If they wish, they can do it themselves with FTP.  Of course, 
I'll take care of the "I didn't know my account was gone because I 
only log in once a month" folks with my tape archives.

I have also used this scheme on those (exceedingly rare - perhaps 4
times) occasions when I have temporarily locked out a user; a different 
message informs them that they need to see me ASAP, and that they can 
use FTP to move their critical files to other machines to continue their 
academic work.  

Is there something wrong/unfair/improper with this scheme?  I've never
heard any complaints, but I'm open to suggestions.

--Wes


