Because we all have a stake in the development of reasonable computer
use policies, and in educating administrators and lawmakers about
computer use to help promote reasonable policies. 
  We all benefit when other sites are well-run. A site with no
policies or discipline can unleash a lot of chaos onto the net. A site
with repressive policies can block the flow of information upstream
and downstream. Finally, we are trying to help the users on other
sites to get reasonable access for their own education and work,
because we are part of a community and we can lend them a hand, if
they have been unjustly treated.

  I don't want to see the net become a dumping ground for every user
who feels that the rules don't apply to him or her... but I do want to
see us help users who are being punished unfairly. 
 
 This particular case has seen a student's academic career severely
damaged for something that his school administration did not recognize
as a trivial and harmless action (IMHO) If a public university can
throw its students out on a whim, we are all in trouble. I work at a
public university myself and I am glad that there is some forum for
public feedback.

Heck, we've thrown one student off of our own system this year, and
put one other on a sort of probation (he can finish his course, he
can't use the system for ANYTHING but course work and the account will
be disabled between semesters. This for repeated violations of our
resource guidelines accompanied by his written refusal to follow the
guidelines -- this particular student didn't grasp that there were
some resources free because we *have* guidelines and ask people to
follow them. He also didn't grasp that he was getting complaints from
me because his activities were swamping the CPU and the network...I
only start monitoring types of use when the load goes too high!) 
  
   Anyway, I don't mind having our actions held to public light. There
is a bottom line:  my bosses set system policy and users have to abide
by it whether they agree or not. However, we are always willing to
talk about it, and users can question our policies either by talking
to us or by going through various university channels (Dept. Chair,
various deans and committees.) Public pressure can be a real factor.
The Internet is a "hot" topic right now and access to the net is
attracting a lot of interest. 

  [Besides, it is rewarding for those of us who try to provide the
most services on a shoestring, to see complaints from users at other
universities. When our users start to complain, as some always will,
it is nice to know how we compare to other schools.]



  






