I am a grad student, and I consider every file which I can
read to be fair game.  That's why they are readable.  In my
own directory, I have subdirectories which only I (and the
admin-gods) can read, and others which anyone can waste
their time perusing if they wish.  Many times I have
executed "find / -name something", hoping that someone
would have what I need.  Sure there are better ways, but
I didn't always know that, and it wouldn't make any difference
if I did.  That is the way it is SUPPOSED to work.

What I have concluded from the discussion, after reading
nearly every related article, is that University of Houston
is a poor choice for a computer science education.  With
facilities so limited and staff so ignorant, I would not
seriously consider any interaction with UH.  I will no doubt
be discussing UH's shortcomings with my peers, children, etc.
Wasted paper, wasted CPU cycles, he MIGHT do something
antisocial: these are justification for banishment?  They have
staff to waste time on this bull?  

UH is out there, they know this discussion is going on, and
they could have said SOMETHING to inform our judgement.
Instead, we get one side, in greater detail and with more
justification each passing day.

This is not a "tempest in a teapot", as has been suggested,
It's about freedom to act without minute supervision.  It's
about prosecution of mythical offenses by narrow minded
beaureaucrats, who make up the rules after the fact.  It's
about incompetence in our universities, which cannot even be
discussed, much less admitted.


