This is getting sort of silly, even if not embarrasingly so...it's
polarizing out to ``I can read any file the system lets me read'' vs
``I have no right to read any file I am not explicitly authorized to
read''.

We're human beings, not automatons, we don't have to run our lives by
boolean algebra.

The file in question was /etc/passwd which, regardless of its
awe-inspiring name, is the most publicly read file on any Unix system.
Period. You can't even login without causing much of it to be read on
your behalf. It's left publicly readable for a reason, the information
contained in it is expected to be read by everyone, constantly,
period.

The specific charge against this student was that he printed out the
passwd file.

I suspect if the file had been called /etc/users instead of
/etc/passwd he'd still be a student there. He's been murdered over a
naming convention, essentially.

As I said before, the system administrator(s) at Univ of Houston who
did not know at the time they brought charges that there was nothing
sensitive in the file should be reprimanded and probably released from
their duties: They are incompetent by any measure, and a clear hazard
to anyone who relies on the services they cash their paychecks to
provide.

The dean should be set out to pasture and not allowed to engage in
such cases again, the total lack of judgement comes through clearly.

The student should be both re-instated and offered an apology from the
university, and damages to the student should be investigated.

Nothing that has come forward convinces me of anything other than the
stunning stupidity and incompetence of the university employees who
caused this mess and have seriously embarrassed their university.

If the university does not clear this matter up then we can only
conclude that the institution is seriously neck-deep in egregious
stupidity, having made a serious mistake the entire lot of them cannot
drag themselves to correct it.

If that is the case then the institution should be shunned for a
period not less than the remainder of what would have been the
student's matriculation (the next three years): Papers should not be
accepted, grants should be disrecommended, alumni should be informed
and advised to withold contributions, etc.

Anyone who stood by and tolerated such injustice should experience
injustice.

Then perhaps they will understand why it is unreasonable to stand by
and delude oneself that justice can be denied to others while assuming
it is still to be extended to themselves. If they turn their backs on
justice let justice turn its back on them.

See you in the "smokey rooms" folks.

