[I must be out of my mind to jump into this, but here goes :-) ]

|Let's clarify one point here. The file involved in this case is
|/etc/passwd. this a publicly accessable file that, in spite of its name,
|NEVER contains cleartext passwords. In this case, since the system used
|password shadowing, there were not even encrypted passwords. 
|
|Again. This is a publicly accessable file. On any UNIX system in the world,
|any user can type
|
|  cat /etc/passwd
|
|a dump it to his screen.

People keep going on about this point.  This is just being pendantic.

/etc/passwd is world-readable because certain system functions depend
on it being so; this isn't a license for anyone to look at it, print
copies, etc.  A system administrator who ignored attempts to copy the
file offsite would be remiss in his duties; it's just prudent
administration.  Even without the passwords, just knowing what accounts
exist is the first step toward breaking into a system, even if you
ignore privacy issues.  A finger daemon is a calculated security risk,
and one that accepts wildcards is not precedent for ignoring other
security holes.

Similarly, one can access passwd through ls & friends because they do
so in a controlled fashion and for a good reason.  This is a completely
different situation from catting or printing the file.  The only way
they *aren't* different is from the purely technical standpoint.  Purely
technical analyses are great for uncovering security holes and the like,
but they're pretty poor grounds for making policy.  If I load a pistol
with blanks, tell you they're real, and ask you kindly for your wallet,
wouldn't you like to have some legal recource against me?  [Let's ignore
the fact that blanks can be dangerous at close range.]

One difference is motive.  Shadow passwords or no, it's reasonable to
be suspicious of someone making a copy of the raw password file.  The
school is allowed to use motive or suspected motive as a factor, just
like juries and parents and everyone else who has to make judgements
about other people.  And they're in a much better position to judge
this person's motives than anyone here is.

Like several other people I have a feeling we haven't heard the whole
story yet.  It's been admitted this isn't the first time the fellow has
run afoul of the administration; it's not unreasonable for them to
adopt harsher punishments for repeated offenders.  And if the
administration felt he was really trying to break security, the fact
that he couldn't have actually done so with the password file is not
grounds for dismissing the complaint.
-- 
