Some reactions to some claims made:

That it is ok to access *any* file whose user permissions allow such
access.
    I think this is a good policy for system files, but not for
    others' user files.

That UH should defend itself on-line.
    The FERPA (now *all* on-line) requires secrecy unless the student
    suspect waives it. On the good side this protects student privacy.
    On the bad side, this means that student discipline is conducted
    via secret trials. Also, universities get used to working in
    secret and are less likely to talk even if the student waives his
    or her "rights".

That if you are smart enough to decipher passwords in /etc/passwd then you
are smart enough to use them wisely.
    I don't believe this. I think deciphering passwords is a
    reasonable thing to prohibit. (In the UH case, however, there were
    no passwords, even encrypted ones, in /etc/passwd.)

That it was suspicious to print the /etc/passwd. Maybe the student
thought there were passwords there and maybe he intended to decypher
them and use them.
    This is all possible, but in there is no preponderate evidence
    (that we know of) that this is true.

- Carl

