Don't bet on it.  Students got into deep trouble over technically trivial
 things at CMU while I was there (graduated '91).  I was working for
 Academic Computing most of my 4 1/2 years there, and had a pretty good
 look at what was behind most of such "prosecutions."  Typically, there'd
 be an individual hacker, who annoyed people in mid-level administrative
 positions with perfectly legit word, who did something that normally would
 be shrugged off.  The administrator would then try to nail the hacker using
 obscure and poorly enforced rules.

One case comes to mind: a manager of one of the local research programming
 groups (I believe its current project was then MacMach) got mail from one
 of the aforementioned hacker-types, telling him that he had stored mail
 containing a password to an administrative account in a publicly readable
 place.  The hacker in question was one of several who annoyed admins by
 creating alternative interfaces to official supported functions: usually
 interfaces that won big in terms of usability and performance.

Instead of thanking him, or even just moving the password, the manager did
 his best to get the student nailed to the wall (it probably had nothing to
 do with the hacker quoting the computing guidelines that instruct people to
 never ever send passwords through the mail).  Fortunately, nothing came of
 it, since student affairs people are more clueful about technical matters
 than they used to be (see below).

This is one of many incident I personally witnessed from the Academic
 Computing side.  Another (the canonical CMU absurd abuse prosecution story)
 incident happened before I got to CMU, when a student created a nested set
 of directories recursively.  This crashed in a fairly nasty way the
 brain-dead system it was runing on: the student (who is now in grad school
 at BU, I believe) was expelled for malicious abuse of facilities, mostly
 because of an instructor who ran the politically important experimental
 program.

I can't speak for MIT or CalTech, but CMU certainly isn't immune to this
 sort of bogosity.  I can certainly see the "KC" sort of thing happening
 to somebody who annoyed the right people with legit behavior.

