I was at CMU during this incident, and I remember MANY people sharing
the opinion that the whole incident would have eventually blown over
with only a slap on the wrist to the offending hacker-kid (who
incidentally did not seem very much like a hacker at all to those that
I knew), had not he and his friends gone to the local news media and
publicized the situation.  Once the s**t hit the fan and the media
were involved, the University administration was put in a position
where they *could* *not* give him anything but expulsion, the maximum
penalty, without appearing to be backing down in a very public way.  I
also know about hackers who have done far worse things in the past,
but kept it all quiet and within the University and got off with much
more lenient punishments, such as a temporary account suspension,
account restrictions or not being allowed to use a particular system,
while maintaining their accounts on other systems.  The lesson of this
particular incident to me seems to be one of those valuable Real World
(TM) lessons, namely that politics counts for a whole lot, and you
should neither try to piss people off unnecessarily nor make
negotiation difficult, e.g.  by going public, except as a last resort.
