Most (if not all) Unix systems allow the user to set the default
permissions on the files they create.  Check out the "umask" command
for more information; it's built into most shells.

>Are you saying that when a mailbox is created, it's created world readable?

No, I'm not saying that at all.  On most Unix systems, mailboxes are
only readable by the owner and the email delivery programs.  I have,
however, seen glitchy email systems that left mailboxes world-readable.
I've also seen inexperienced users chmod their mailbox.........one fel-
low thought that it had to be world-*writable* in order to get his mail.  8(

>If someone makes a file world readable, they most likely want the world
>to read it.

I support the students/faculty of a College of Engineering.  Since
engineering naturally involves group work, most users leave their
files world-readable; the alternative is some massive work with
chmod and email tag with "ok, you can get that file now" and "I
got it; chmod it back" messages.  We've worked on an honor system;
it's simply understood that you don't browse other people's direc-
tories/files without their permission (and I mention it in every
introductory lecture).  The honor system seems to be working; we've 
never been involved in an online plaigiarism case, and the (small 
minority of) experienced users chown their stuff themselves.

After watching an honor system work for years upon years, people
like the "hey, I can browse any darned thing I please" poster
above really touch an open nerve with me.......it's just common
courtesy to ask permission.

