The issues are simple:

1) a computer use policy exists
2) the student agreed, in writing, to abide by that policy
3) the student violated section 1 of said policy

Forget the name of the file, that is unimportant.
Forget the fact that the file was world readable, that is unimportant.

The issue is, an agreement was broken. This is an agreement that was entered 
into by an individual who had a choice. The individual could have chosen to not 
enter into this agreement. Once the student agreed to the rules, then they had 
to abide by that agreement.

In society you make numerous compacts. If I agree to borrow money from you and 
buy a car, and then pay you back at a monthly rate, does that change if the car 
is stollen? No, it doesn't. I still have to live up to my part of the 
agreement.

The agreement that the school uses may be antiquated. It may be bad. It may 
even be questionable in a legal sense, but it is the agreement that they hold 
all students to. It is the agreement that all students *must* agree to in order 
to use the facilities.

Once a student agree to these rules, they are to be governed by them. One has 
to ask, "how many other students violate these rules?" One? 100? If other 
students could follow these rules, why couldn't this one?

I agree, when we see bad policy we should do all we can to help straighten out 
the policy makers. In this case the policy has areas that could be considered 
vague. But, once you agree to live in a dictatorship, you have to live by the 
rules of the dictator.

Now, the punishment issue is another story completly. But, without all the 
facts, how can one really know if it was excessive or not?

