There is a principle of law that applies in the US: if you didn't lock it up,
then it's okay for people to enter.  If you fence your property, then
people aren't allowed on it, if you lock your house's doors, then people
aren't allowed in.  If you _don't_ do either of these, then people _are_
allowed on your lawn, and they _are_ allowed in your house - though it
is still theft if they take anything, they cannot be charged with trespass.
Nor will insurance companies reimburse you for any theft under such cir-
cumstances, since you did not take prudent action in protecting your
valuables.  In fact, if you _don't_ put up a fence and _successfully_
keep people off your property, many states will, after some period of
time, declare an easement which will give people a _legal_right_ to be
on your property.  In at least one case in NH, the property owner was
required to tear down a house he built across a path that crossed his
property connecting the country club next door with the lake they both
shared.  They had previously torn _down_ his fences, but the law does
not recognize _attempts_ to prevent trespass, it only acknowledges that
you have prevented trespass and therefore assert your rights to prevent
formation of an easement.

This applies to computers as well, which is why when you log into most
systems now you get that silly message about unlawful use or entry.  If
the university hadn't wanted people to read the passwd file, they should
have used the facilities of the operating system to ensure that students
did not have the privileges to do so.  It's simple enough.  Instead, they
left the door unlocked and open, and then suspended someone for wandering
in.  It won't wash in a court of law, nor should it in a university
proceeding.

I would be real annoyed about this, too, except that I long ago came
to the conclusion that universities are legal rip-off institutions,
who provide no guarantees, nor any quality control of their product,
and which believe themselves exempt from any responsibility to anyone.
People who subject themselves to such conditions get what they deserve.
You have to learn to _expect_ them to be run by fools or charletons,
and act accordingly.

I expect admin got all bent out of shape about reading the _password_
file.  Never mind that even in older Unix systems that information is
encrypted and useless for illegal entry unless people are using really
stupid passwords.  Most Unix systems nowadays don't have even the encrypted
passwords in the "passwd" file any more.  As long as write access is
controlled there is really no more need to protect it than /etc/fstab.
If admin felt otherwise then they should have done it - and if they
decided otherwise only _after_ what's-his-name tried to print it then
they should have simply let it go.  Crimes cannot be legislated retro-
actively in this country.  If it was legal when the action occurred, then
that action will always _be_ legal, even if made illegal later.  This is
not Germany, where people can be convicted of crimes that hadn't been
invented at the time the "illegal" action occurred, and who can be punished
for doing what was _required_ under penalty of law by their government at
the time.  _Civilized_ nations have ethical standards for what is criminal
and what is not.

