In article <1u2qqoINN559@twain.ucs.umass.edu> mrunkel@twain.ucs.umass.edu (Marc A. Runkel) says:
: Huh? I was my understanding that a unix system defaults to only allowing
: the creator of a file to read it.  Are you saying that when a mailbox
: is created, it's created world readable?

Mail files are created readable only by the user.  The default permissions
on a file you create depend upon your umask setting.  That can default
to anything (and usually defaults to world readable unless you or your
system administrator changes it).

We had a user who got a lot of email (apparently hundreds of messages)
that he wanted to get rid of.  A "expert" in his department told him
that he could remove the messages much faster by removing the file
/usr/spool/mail/hisuserid and then doing a touch to create it empty
(the "expert" thought that the file had to exist).  Guess what that
did?

I just checked our spool.  We have 1889 mailbox files in our spool
directory.  Three of them are world readable.  Two of those are world
writeable.

: If someone makes a file world readable, they most likely want the world
: to read it.

Don't bet on it.  You'd be amazed at the things users will do in the
course of trying to figure out how to share files or make their .plan
files readable by the finger daemon.  I can't make users pass a test
before they get an account.  I can't make them consult user services
instead of some "expert" in the department before running commands.  I
can keep an eye on things and send warnings when I see something
dangerous but thats about it.

I want users to be willing to explore and try to learn.  If we operate
under the assumption that any mistake they make is "intentional" then
they can't do that.
