: read to be fair game.  That's why they are readable.

In the case of system files this is reasonable.  The administrator
of the system should know enough to control sensitive files.

But users make mistakes and don't often know the implications of what
they are doing.  Novices have trouble understanding chmod and often
make files readable when they only need to be executable (execute
permissions on directories are an even harder concept to grasp).  They
even make files writable when they only intended to make them
readable.

Users explor and try to figure out how to make their .plan files
readable or share a particular file with a particular friend or what
have you.  In the process they make mistakes.  It isn't "right" or
"fair" for you to take advantage of their mistakes to paw through their
private files without explicit permission.
