there were people watching to make sure it did not get out of hand,
and it never did) to encourage experimentation. Lots of nasty things
happened that day, but everyone had a good time and learned from it.
Then the system was restored to proper status.

There were cases of creative things being done (mostly exploiting
Multics' tremendous capabilities) including multi-player dungeon games
(in PL/1 :-)), a few innocent pranks tricks with escape sequences on
the televideos), and maybe once somebody figured out how to crash
Multics. But the computer center people took it all quite well, and
many VERY knowledgeable people came out of it.

Even when we moved to Unix and relatively unstable hardware (leading edge :-))
and not-so-creative ways of crashing the system were found (adb on a
Pyramid, the recursive create dirs program till filesystem runs out of
inodes, while(1) fork(), etc...) again no major problems. Only a note
on the bulletin board "If you know how to crash the pyramid using adb,
please let us know :-)"...

On the grad student machine, source was accessible again, as were all
manuals. I remember my first introduction to Unix being figuring out
how to PRINT the stuff (what is 'tbl' anyway :-)). Then came source
code reading rituals, Rogomatic, Ingres, the works. I think I ended up
being better off having done things like that... And I still remember
the sysadmins of both the Multics and Unix systems, and thank them for
giving us the opportunity to experiment, and certainly learn more in
the process.

The admins occasionally pointed out no-no things to us but I do not
recall any witch-hunts like the ones described here. From what I am
reading here we had it real good. 

So not all educational insttitutions are like UH or GMU... But it is a
scary thought that more and more institutions are moving in UH's 
direction.

