A sidenote to the chaos caused by incompetence at UH.

I had the luxury of attending The University of Houston for approximately
two semesters.  I was thoroughly unimpressed with both the quality of
education provided and the reactionary behavior of the Computer Center Staff.

At the time I was a student I was also working as a computer security 
consultant.  Upon my entrance to UH I immediately filled out the necessary
paperwork to receive accounts on the VMS Cluster and on the Ultrix that
were available for general student use.

During the course of my regular career, I happened across a program that
allowed one to view the contents of kmem through a bug existing in several
versions of Ultrix.  I decided that it would be good to know if the problem
existed in the newest version of Ultrix which was running on the machine
"Menudo" on which I had an account.  The program was invoked by shelling out
through /bin/mail and spawning a background process that read kmem into a file.
It worked.  I then killed my processes and called a few clients to let them 
know of the existence in current versions and told them the proper steps
to take to correct it.  I even called Ed DiHart at Cert to mention it to him.

Two days later, I logged into Menudo to find that I had been given a noshell.
I called the computation center and was told that I must contact Howard Jares
to discuss my account.  After a week of trying, I finally reached Mr. Jares,
who told me that his staff had seen me trying to sabotage the unix and that
I was in complete violation of Computer Use Policy.  I told Mr. Jares
what had happened and expected him to understand.  I was wrong.

Jares was of the mindset that Unix were large natives who were no longer 
capable of reproducing.  I tried again to explain that the system was never
in any danger, and in fact, I told him the proper permissions to set /bin/mail
as a work around.  He stated that he would not authorize my account being turned
back on, but I should talk to the admins and see what they would advise.

I then called Alan Pfeiffer-Traum, then sysadm of Menudo.  Alan was aware of
me and my company, since he had set up Menudo to give us USENET feeds
only a few weeks prior.  I told Alan the problem and what had really happened.
Alan was only slightly more understanding.  He said that by reading kmem
I could have potentially gotten people's passwords.  I said of course I could have, which is precisely why I needed to see if this bug worked.  I told him
of a fix, but he did not budge.  I said to him, If I had actually wanted
too I could have root on menudo, so for me to waste time grabbing
users passwords by grepping through kmem dumps was ludicrous.  He said he
believed me but would not tell Jares that it would be ok to reactivate my 
account.

I called Jares back some time later, and he agreed to let me keep my VMS 
account.  (Not as bad as expulsion but damn near...a semester of VMS)

I then told Jares that since I wasn't able to use the facilities, that
I would like my computer use fees refunded immediately.  He said that
was out of the question.  He then went on to tell me that under current
guidelines, anyone using UH machines for irc, news, email, etc... were
misusing the facilities, but at present these were being excused.  FTP
and telnetting around the Internet were also violations, since very rarely
would the fall into the requirements of classes.

I was appalled.

This is what every student who attends UH has to deal with.  It comes as
no surprise that someone got expelled for cat-ing a password file, 
shadowed or otherwise.  They are just that clueless and reactionary.

Put aside the fact that far more valuable information for a would be
intruder can be obtained from anywhere on the net by doing a 
finger @@menudo.uh.edu and getting the full finger info on EVERY user
on the machine.  

It's flat out disgusting, and my sympathies go out to Mr. Henderson and
everyone else who has to suffer through their education at UH.

->ME

