If KC did what he and his friend *claim* he did, I would have done this:

	o  Stop the print job.
	o  Confiscate the printed copy.
	o  Kill any active processes under his user identification.
	o  Disable interactive logins for his user identification.
	o  Present a "come see me" type message to him when he tries to
	   log in.
	o  When/if he arrives, tell him that printing /etc/passwd is in
	   violation of the "intended use" policy.
	o  Request an explaination.
	o  If explaination seems reasonable then
		- Retain and/or destroy printed copy of /etc/passwd
		- Verbally issue explicit prohibition on copying or
		  printing any files outside his directory tree that are
		  not specifically by him.
		- Enable logins for his user identification.
		- Allow him to continue removing his files.
	   otherwise, or if no explaination is offered
		- Retain and/or destroy printed copy of /etc/passwd
		- Verbally reprimand him for his misuse of the system
		- Offer to personally archive, download, or print his
		  files and then do so if he so desires.  Note that he
		  would be allowed to monitor and direct my actions in
		  this regard; also, he would need to provide his own
		  magnetic media if that was where he wanted his files.
	o  If he has a record of system abuse (as KC did), I would suspend
	   as much of his resource access as possible for either 1) the
	   summer term; 2) the fall semester; or 3) both, depending on
	   whether he would be around during the summer, and how much of a
	   record he had.  Otherwise, after his files had been removed, he
	   no suspension would be given (although his account would still be
	   disabled since he was no longer in the class for which it was
	   issued).

Given the circumstances presented to us, I would not have taken the matter to
any higher authority.

