Jack Mcbryde writes:
[...]
>According to these rules 'ls', 'tar', 'cd', and 'man' are also illegal to
>use. Given the likelihood that most people who use the system have used one
>of these commands at some time, "selective" is quite accurate. And god
>forbid anyone print out the man pages for tar!

This is actually a very good, valid point.  Technically, none of these
commands have any direct relation to the three explicitly allowed functions
(text processing, electronic mail, USENET news).  However, because the three
allowed functions provide certain services, it is reasonable to assume that
system services necessary to take advantage of them are also allowed.

For instance, some mailers will create and/or use a specific mail directory.
Should you care to edit the text of some of your messages, you would have to
get to them.  Changing into a subdirectory, editing a file, then changing
back to your original directory is functionally equivalent to indicating the
full path of the file when invoking the editor.  Newsreaders function
similarly.  In addition, they save files to a subdirectory (or within a sub-
directory tree).  Accessing those files could also be done the same way.
Therefore, cd is allowed.

Other arguments could be made for other commands, but I don't have the time
now, and really don't care to bother.  I *would* consider the particular
wording of the policies in this regard to be prohibitive, though.  So, now
that a problem with them has been identified, the UH user-base should rally
to have them *changed*, rather than simply violating the rules that they
agreed to in the first place...

-Steve Coile

