Actually, the library/encyclopaedia analogy brings up a good point,
though not the one the original poster wanted to make:

For years I have felt that computing services at most universities
belongs under their library administration (I did several years in the
University sysadmin thing.)

The critical aspect of information science etc is probably more
important to the academic mission, ultimately, than applying software
updates and doing backups etc which is (relatively) more a
"janitorial" function. Most universities are confused, it's almost as
if they decided that because you can't use a computer unless it's
plugged in they'd better put the electricians in charge of academic
computing services.

Someone here described their academic computing services head as a
"former fortran mainframe hacker". Wow! (he says sarcastically), what
a *great* reason to put him in charge of all the information systems!
I sure hope he has something else to his credit besides knowing where
column 6 is! (maybe he does, but...)

One just has to look at how poorly most systems' information files
(mine included) are organized to understand what a bad idea it is to
have untrained people dabble in this sort of thing in their spare
time.

But perhaps more critically, many of these issues relating to policies
regarding information access and distribution are something which
librarians as a profession have dealt with for, literally, hundreds of
years. It's hard stuff, simply setting reasonable standards is hard
stuff.

I couldn't imagine a librarian getting a student suspended because he
copied something that was sitting on a shelf, the image is laughably
ludicrous. Or being easily bullied into removing "sensitive" files.

I'm quite serious. As soon as possible I hope to hire a person with a
good background in library science here at World and try to get a lot
of things back on track, this is not a job for amateurs.

I hope someone out there in university-land realizes that what I say
has merit. Maybe 20 years ago when 95% of the job of adminstering
computing systems was highly technical and you really only dealt with
a small and focused technical community (engineers, scientists, etc)
it made sense to have the whole show run by technical people.

But today's issues; information organization, access policies,
censorship, copyrights, acquisitions, public access, etc, are far more
suited to people with library training than people who know how to
install software, the latter is just an infrastructure function, not
an academic one.

This example at the University of Houston is not isolated, it's really
quite a common sort of thing, and just further indication that the
wrong people have been put at the wheel.


