you to them.  They aren't necessarily going to be predisposed to let you go
so easily.

In case two, maybe the head of the State Police had State Police business to
attend to, in which case he *should* get off (assuming it was a legitimate
excuse to speed).  Otherwise, people would have a right to be upset.  I'm not
aware of the circumstances of the case, so I can so no more.

> SC> If he thought the rules were inappropriate and/or unjust when
> SC> presented with them, he shouldn't have agreed to abide be them.  *I*
> SC> certainly wouldn't have.
>
>He had a choice?  He could sign, or he could not have an account.  It's
>like those non-competition clauses in some contracts, "You can't work in
>this field for 10 years after you leave here."

He could attend another school.  He could resign himself to following the
rules.  Whatever the case, if he had no intention of following the rules, he
should not have signed the paper.

> SC> Refer to my encyclopedia analogy.  I think it better demonstrates my
> SC> point.
>
>Encyclopedias are copyrighted.  Simple directories are not (they cannot be).

Ah, but they *do* belong to someone, and that someone has the right to limit
the use of the contents.  In this case, the directories and the file in
question belonged (i.e. was "owned" in the legal sense) by the University.  As
such, they have the right to control access to it determine appropriate use.

> SC> Let's see: the passwd file contains (to my understanding -- I'm not a
> SC> Unix fan in the least), in addition to otherwise publically accessable
> SC> information (e.g. through finger), your user-id and group-id, and "at
> SC> worst", encrypted passwords.
>
>Right, but in the UH case, it contained no encrypted passwords.
>
>ls -ln, on some systems (on SunOS 4.x systems, /usr/5bin/ls -ln) will give
>user-id and group-id for a file.  Using that on people's home directories
>would be fairly simple.

But hey, looking at other's home directories would *also* be a violation of
the rules, *and* it would be more clear-cut than printing /etc/passwd.

> SC> Knowing a list of usernames and their associated encrypted passwords,
> SC> I could very easily match their encrypted passwords against a list of
> SC> previously encrypted common words, names, and other text strings to
> SC> find matches.  Then, by cross-referencing the encrypted password
> SC> against the related list of unencrypted words, I discover the user's
> SC> password, and summarily have access to their account.
>
>I see.  And you do this from a paper copy of the passwd file?  In
>particular, a paper copy that has no encrypted passwords?

Say you have a preencrypted list of common words, names, and other text
strings, and you have an idea of how frequently certain passwords occur
over others.  Knowing, or having a good idea which accounts have privileges
would provide you with a good set of targets for a brute-force crack attempt.

-Steve Coile

