In article <BZS.93May29190101@world.std.com> bzs@world.std.com (Barry Shein) says:
: 
: For years I have felt that computing services at most universities
: belongs under their library administration (I did several years in the
: University sysadmin thing.)

In policy terms thats probably a good idea.  You still need people who
know how to spec a system to serve X thousand users and people who know
how to determine whether a slow system needs faster CPUs or more disk
controllers and to develop account maintenance software and the like.
We also do things like design and manage the campus network and public
lab facilities.  Much of our work is "janitorial" in nature...but much
is also at the "plumber" and occasionally at the "architect" level.

And there are aspects of policy making in computing that simply have no
analog in libraries (remember computers are still used for things like
number crunching and teaching programing even though these are no
longer their only or even their most important functions).  There is
still an important place for computer knowledgable people even in
policy making areas.

: Someone here described their academic computing services head as a
: "former fortran mainframe hacker". Wow! (he says sarcastically), what
: a *great* reason to put him in charge of all the information systems!
: I sure hope he has something else to his credit besides knowing where
: column 6 is! (maybe he does, but...)

That would be me. And yes, he does have other qualifications.  Like
knowing how to spec a system to serve...  :-)

He and the head of our library now answer to the same boss by the way.
Hopefully that will lead to merging of efforts (in both directions,
they have a UNIX based card catalog system and a Novell based CDROM
system and their own technical people to run them).

: One just has to look at how poorly most systems' information files
: (mine included) are organized to understand what a bad idea it is to
: have untrained people dabble in this sort of thing in their spare
: time.

We are in the process of officially developing a gopher service.  Rather
than try to design the menus ourselves we created a graduate assistantship
position and hired an English major.  This seems to be proving a reasonable
division of labor.

My personal dream is of a campus in which Printing Services (the people
who print up business cards or letterhead or envelops or deal with
special printing needs) manage public print facilities (in public
student labs and the like) and the Library is in charge of things like
gopher and usenet and Coputing Services spec, purchase and manage
computing resources for and between all of them.  Things like general
student computing facilities would probably require some sort of joint
authority.

: I couldn't imagine a librarian getting a student suspended because he
: copied something that was sitting on a shelf, the image is laughably
: ludicrous. Or being easily bullied into removing "sensitive" files.

Actually, its sad how often this turns out to be untrue.  Read any
reference on book banning and you'll be saddened by how many times
texts have been pulled from library shelves because of pressure by
various right wing and PC interest groups.  A lot of libraries don't
carry Playboy (a magazine which should be carried on the merits of its
interviews column alone) or Madonna's book.  When I was growing up in
PG County, Maryland a librarian there wouldn't let me check out
_Stranger_in_a_Strange_Land_ until my mother told her it was all
right.

Still, librarians would probably be an improvement in most cases.
