Read permission implies (surprise!) that those who have such permission
may read the file.  Sorry to break the news.

No, a desk or file cabinet is not the same as a file in a file system,
much as old folks would like to think.  A desk or file cabinet is big
and heavy.  It's hard to write a shell script which will list all the
items starting with "A" in a desk or file cabinet.  A desk or file cabinet
requires some physical activity: moving into someone's space, reaching for
the drawer, peering in, etc.

To look for something in a file system, one need only type:

 find <top of search> -name <something>

When someone executes this command s/he does not even know where it will
look!  It's quite a bit different from going thru desks.  If I was to go
thru desks, presumably I would only go thru those on which I had permission.
Similarly, the find command will go only to those places where I have read
permission.  To search places other than those for which I have read
permission is an attack.  To search places for which I have read permission
is an intelligent use of the resources available to me.

Permission is given by the little 'r' in the directory listing.  I am a
(not so) humble grad student, not a Sysadmin God, but this much I know.
That's how it works.

