Thus said scoile@gmuvax2.gmu.edu (Steve Coile):
>>Encyclopedias are copyrighted.  Simple directories are not (they cannot be).
>
>Ah, but they *do* belong to someone, and that someone has the right to limit
>the use of the contents.  In this case, the directories and the file in
>question belonged (i.e. was "owned" in the legal sense) by the University.  As
>such, they have the right to control access to it determine appropriate use.

That someone has the right to limit the use of the contents through a
mechanism called "copyright".  This mechanism does not seem to apply
readily here, and no attempt was made to apply it anyway.  Can you
tell me what it means to say "there is no copyright on this, it's in
the public domain, but it's owned by someone who can nail you if you
try to make a printout of it"?

Suppose I have a car parked in my driveway, and you walk by and take a
photograph of it.  Do I have the right to control your access to that
photograph because the information in it (i.e. my car) is owned by me?
Do I have the right to ban you from ever using a camera again, and
forbid you to walk down the street for a year?  Is it reasonable for
me to claim that you were obviously taking a photo of it because you
are planning to steal it later and wanted to get a picture of how the
ignition mechanism works so you can hotwire it (even though that isn't
shown in the picture)?

>>I see.  And you do this from a paper copy of the passwd file?  In
>>particular, a paper copy that has no encrypted passwords?
>
>Say you have a preencrypted list of common words, names, and other text
>strings, and you have an idea of how frequently certain passwords occur
>over others.  Knowing, or having a good idea which accounts have privileges
>would provide you with a good set of targets for a brute-force crack attempt.

Ever heard of "salts"?  No?  I didn't think so.  The concept of using
a "preencrypted list of common words" to try to break >1 encrypted
password is absurd (and irrelevant, since there were no encrypted
passwords in that file anyway.)

Of course, if he had really been interested in cracking accounts, he
should have been using the "legitimate system tool" of finger(1) which
will provide other useful information such as last login time which
can allow a cracker to locate unused accounts (which are more likely
to have weak passwords and less likely to be detected as broken.)
